  data: {
    LLM: "Trust the math! With a 52-card deck and 5 players (25 cards total), the 'Average' amount of any one rank plus wilds is about 3. If someone bids 'Five 9s', they are likely bluffing unless they hold two 9s themselves. Always check your own hand first; if you have zero Jacks, your opponent's bid of 'Four Jacks' is high-risk.\n\nThe 'Corner' Play: If you have a horrible hand (no pairs, no Aces), you MUST bluff early to push the bid up highly enough that the next player feels forced to call Dudo on the NEXT person. Bid small on a rank you don't have to 'Seed' the idea that you are strong, then Dudo your opponent when they try to overreach.\n\nJoker-Ace Tracking: Keep track of how many Aces (wilds) have been discarded from the hands of eliminated players. If 3 Aces are gone, the 'Float' of the game drops significantly. A bid of 'Five Kings' becomes almost impossible without those wilds. The late-game is purely about card-counting the remaining deck potential.",
    Player: "Trust the math! With a 52-card deck and 5 players (25 cards total), the 'Average' amount of any one rank plus wilds is about 3. If someone bids 'Five 9s', they are likely bluffing unless they hold two 9s themselves. Always check your own hand first; if you have zero Jacks, your opponent's bid of 'Four Jacks' is high-risk.\n\nThe 'Corner' Play: If you have a horrible hand (no pairs, no Aces), you MUST bluff early to push the bid up highly enough that the next player feels forced to call Dudo on the NEXT person. Bid small on a rank you don't have to 'Seed' the idea that you are strong, then Dudo your opponent when they try to overreach.\n\nJoker-Ace Tracking: Keep track of how many Aces (wilds) have been discarded from the hands of eliminated players. If 3 Aces are gone, the 'Float' of the game drops significantly. A bid of 'Five Kings' becomes almost impossible without those wilds. The late-game is purely about card-counting the remaining deck potential.",
